      'Avoid swapping automatically every turn. Sometimes the current bubble can create a better opening even if the next one looks easier. Ask whether the shot will remove a support, connect a pair or create an accessible route. If it does none of those things, using the swap is a sensible way to keep the board manageable.',
      '## Build the bomb and use rainbow bubbles',
      'Every five shots without a pop fills the meter and makes the next bubble a bomb. The bomb can help when ordinary colours do not make a safe match, but it is better to pop groups before the meter forces the field to advance. A rainbow bubble takes the colour of the bubble it lands next to, which makes the landing position especially important.',
      'Do not treat special bubbles as a substitute for aiming. A bomb used on an unhelpful area may not create enough space, and a rainbow bubble can inherit a colour that has no nearby match. Use the guide line to choose the contact point and check the surrounding group before committing.',
      '## Keep the field away from the ring',
      'After several shots without a pop, another layer creeps in around the hole. The red arc on the ring shows how close the danger is. If any bubble touches the ring, the game ends. This makes clearing at least one group more important than spending several turns on a perfect shot.',
      'When the arc is nearly full, prefer a reliable match over a complicated bank shot. If the current layout has no easy pair, look for the largest connected group you can remove or use the swap to find a colour that fits. A small pop that resets pressure can give you room to plan a more ambitious shot next.',
      '## Aim bank shots with purpose',
      'A wall bounce is useful when the direct route is blocked. Aim at the side wall at a shallow angle and follow the dotted path to see where the bubble will arrive. The rebound can reach a matching group behind the front layer, but a small angle change can send it into a different cluster.',
      'Treat each bounce as a geometry problem. Identify the target group, estimate which wall point opens a route and check the end of the preview line. If you are unsure, move the aim slowly instead of firing several test shots. For more examples, read the [Bubble Surge bank shot guide](/blogs/bubble-surge-bank-shots).',
      '## A safe opening routine',
      'At the start of a board, scan for pairs of matching bubbles that are easy to reach. Use one shot to make a pop, then inspect what fell and which routes opened. Try not to fill every gap with an unmatched colour. A stranded bubble can become a blocker and make later shots harder to reach.',
      'If there is no immediate match, look for a shot that connects to a matching colour or clears a support near the edge. Save the swap for a colour that gives a clear improvement. The aim is to keep the field open, reduce the number of isolated groups and avoid long stretches without a pop.',
      '## Touch and keyboard controls',
      'On touch screens, drag a finger to aim and release to shoot. On a computer, move the mouse to aim and click to fire. Left and right arrows adjust the aim, Enter fires and Space swaps bubbles. P pauses the game. Choose the control style that lets you make small angle changes without losing sight of the target.',
      'Mobile players should avoid covering the target with a hand while dragging. Move the finger to aim, check the dotted guide, then release. Keyboard players can nudge the direction one step at a time. Either way, pause when the field is calm enough to inspect a bank shot rather than rushing through a difficult layout.',
      '## Improve one decision at a time',
